  "question": {
    "text": "Which of the following complex is octahedral, diamagnetic and the most stable?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "hxKZP",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>CoCl</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>]</mo><mo>&#160;</mo></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "E2zbi",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mi>Ni</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "12eSf",
      "text": "&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mi>Co</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>CN</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "Giiub",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mi>Co</mi><msub><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The coordination compound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>Co</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>CN</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>]</mo></math>contains a cobalt(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>III</mi></math>) ion, which has a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></math> electronic configuration. The cyanide ligands in this complex are indeed strong field ligands, which means they will cause a large splitting of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></math>orbitals. So here cobalt is in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></math>&nbsp;state having&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></math>&nbsp;configuration and also cyanide ligand which is a strong field ligand so, it is most stable and&nbsp;will have octahedral shape. The given&nbsp;compounds have the following nature:</p><p>(1) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>CoCl</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>]</mo><mo>&#160;</mo></math> &ndash; Paramagnetic</p><p>(2) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Ni</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> &ndash; Paramagnetic</p><p>(3) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Co</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>CN</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math>&ndash; Diamagnetic</p><p>(4) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Co</mi><msub><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> &ndash; Paramagnetic</p></blockquote>",
    "image": null
